The macOS computer-use feature's optional background-window click path
(src/kiro_crew/computer_use/macos_skylight.py) uses undocumented macOS
window-server interfaces. The symbol declarations and the event-field
recipe are derived from prior MIT-licensed open-source projects that
reverse-engineered that path. No third-party code is copied; the
implementation is independent, and the module is isolated so the
dependency is auditable in one place.

The shell-command classifier behind the chat transcript's tool-call titles
(website/src/utils/shellCommandParse.ts and its Python mirror,
src/kiro_crew/tool_call_title_shell_parse.py and
src/kiro_crew/tool_call_title_shell_classify.py, behind
src/kiro_crew/tool_call_title.py) reimplements the rule set of OpenAI
Codex's parse_command.rs (https://github.com/openai/codex, Apache-2.0) in
TypeScript and Python. No Rust source is copied; the Apache-2.0 notice for
Codex is reproduced under "Ported rule sets" in THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES.

The browser tool's fast loop (src/kiro_crew/browser_harness/snapshot_walker.js,
reflex_space.py and reflex_realtime.py) adapts the element table, target guard
and operation/target decision design of browser-use/jev-ultrafast (MIT,
Copyright (c) 2026 Browser Use) and reimplements the pipelined decision clock of
hosseintoussi/jev-flappy-bird (MIT, Copyright (c) 2026 Hossein Toussi). Neither project is a dependency; their MIT
notices are reproduced under "Ported rule sets" in THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES.

